<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benin Bronzes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronzes</a> are thousands of cast metal plaques, heads and sculptures, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and other objects, made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> working for the royal court of the Benin Empire. The oldest date from the 14th century and most from the 15th and 16th, long before the British expedition that looted them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> in 1897; late-19th-century scholars wrongly credited the craft to contact with the Portuguese. Celebrated as masterpieces of African art, they are held largely in Western museums and are the subject of ongoing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> debates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes.jpg" alt="Benin Bronzes" width="302" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benin_brass_plaque_03_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Benin Bronzes decorated the royal palace of the Kingdom of Benin, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. The metal plaques, called Ama in the Edo language, were produced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of Benin Bronze Casters, now located in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-street">Igun Street</a>, and depict scenes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> from the history of the kingdom. Despite the name, the pieces are mostly brass, an alloy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, zinc and lead in varying proportions, made by lost-wax casting.</p><p>Others are of wood, ceramic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, leather or cloth. The brass came partly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manillas</a>, brass bracelets bought from the Portuguese, whose metal is now thought to have come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. Two golden ages of the craft are placed under the obas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Esigie</a>, around 1550, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eresoyen">Eresoyen</a>, in 1735–1750.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Property in bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> was reserved to the highest ranks. In general only the oba could own objects of bronze or ivory, though he could allow high-ranking people to use items such as hanging masks and cuffs. Coral was also a royal material, and coral neck rings, granted by the oba, marked nobility. The plaques were fixed to the palace pillars, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> visitor described galleries on wooden pillars covered with cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> engraved with pictures of war exploits.</p><p>2 mm thick, and the backgrounds of most are incised with the ebe-ame, or river leaf, design, whose leaves were used in healing rites by priestesses of Olokun, the god of the sea. The descendants of the casters still revere Igue-Igha, who introduced casting to the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In January 1897 a party of unarmed British envoys and many African bearers was massacred, and British forces took most of the plaques in the Benin Expedition of that year, which British sources presented as retaliation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> received a large share, about 900 objects today, and others went to museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Before then Europeans had described African works as curiosities of pagan cults, and the late 19th-century scholars O. M. Dalton and Charles Hercules Read mistakenly thought the knowledge of metalworking came from the Portuguese.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> has sought the return of the bronzes since independence in 1960. In 2021 the German government declared it would return looted bronzes in its public collections, and in November 2022 the Horniman Museum became the first museum in the United Kingdom to return bronzes. On 19 June 2025 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> returned 119 bronzes, the largest single return to that date, and in November 2022 the Digital Benin database was launched, listing more than 5,000 artefacts held by over 130 institutions in 20 countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benin Bronzes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of the metal for the <b>Benin Bronzes</b> came from Portuguese brass manillas, horseshoe-shaped bracelets used as currency in trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Former colonial powers have begun to acknowledge past crimes, as Germany did for the Herero and Nama in 2021, and the return of objects taken in colonial wars, such as the <b>Benin Bronzes</b>, has become a public question across Europe.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Erediauwa reigned from 1979 and Ewuare II from 2016, amid campaigns that have brought the return of some <b>Benin Bronzes</b> looted in 1897.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-benin-restored">Kingdom of Benin (restored)</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Ralph Moor, commissioner from 1896, an expedition in 1897 avenged the killing of acting consul James Phillips by sacking Benin City, ending the independence of the Benin Empire and seizing the <b>Benin Bronzes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
